INT. GOAT YOGA BARN - MORNING

A sun-struck barn with rubber mats on the floor and bleating goats everywhere. Hand-lettered sign: "NAMASTE, KIDS." A speakerphone propped on a hay bale.

HORTENSE PLUMB, 70, purple leggings, silver braid, folded into a perfect downward dog, a goat standing serenely on her lower back. She has the stillness of a tax auditor and the patience of a glacier.

CLEMENT (V.O.)
(speakerphone, tinny)
Mother, are you doing yoga with farm animals again?

HORTENSE
It is goat yoga, Clement. The goat is the instructor.

CLEMENT (V.O.)
(speakerphone)
The goat is standing on you.

HORTENSE
Correct.

CLEMENT (V.O.)
Mother, we have discussed this.

HORTENSE
Clement, we have discussed nothing. You talk and I wait.

CLEMENT (V.O.)
I'm worried about the knees.

HORTENSE
The knees are at peace.

The goat shifts. Hortense does not.

CLEMENT (V.O.)
(speakerphone)
Mother, I'm coming over.

HORTENSE
Clement, no.

CLEMENT (V.O.) ^
Mother, yes.

The goat hops off her back. Hortense unfolds one vertebra at a time, stands, turns to the phone with magnificent calm, and, as she answers, the goat chews the hem of her leggings.

HORTENSE
Then bring a carrot.
